\section{The RK4 physics step}\label{app:rk4}

This appendix sets out the scheme used for the Navier--Stokes physics operator
$\Phi^{(\mathrm{P})}$ of \S\ref{ssec:ns-physics-step}: one step of the classical
fourth-order Runge--Kutta method. Throughout we abbreviate the step as
$h \equiv \Delta t$, reserving $\Delta$ for the filter width of the subgrid
stress model of \S\ref{ssec:ns-viscosity}.

\subsection{The scheme}\label{ssec:rk4-scheme}

Written out mode by mode, the physics flow \eqref{eq:ns-physics-flow} is the
system of ordinary differential equations
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rk4-rhs}
  \frac{\mathrm{d}\hat{\tilde u}_i}{\mathrm{d}t} = R_i(\hat{\tilde u}),
  \qquad
  R_i(\hat{\tilde u}) = -P_{il}(\boldsymbol{\kappa}) \left( \hat{N}_l - \hat{G}_l + \hat{F}_l \right) ,
\end{equation}
the bracketed physical terms of \eqref{eq:ns-fourier-final}. RK4 advances it
through four evaluations of $R$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rk4-stages}
  \begin{aligned}
    \hat{k}^{(1)}_i &= R_i\bigl(\hat{\tilde u}^{(n)}\bigr), &
    \hat{k}^{(2)}_i &= R_i\bigl(\hat{\tilde u}^{(n)} + \tfrac{h}{2}\hat{k}^{(1)}\bigr), \\
    \hat{k}^{(3)}_i &= R_i\bigl(\hat{\tilde u}^{(n)} + \tfrac{h}{2}\hat{k}^{(2)}\bigr), &
    \hat{k}^{(4)}_i &= R_i\bigl(\hat{\tilde u}^{(n)} + h\,\hat{k}^{(3)}\bigr),
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
combined with Simpson weights,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rk4-update}
  \hat{\tilde u}_i^{(n+1)} = \hat{\tilde u}_i^{(n)}
    + \frac{h}{6}\left( \hat{k}^{(1)}_i + 2\hat{k}^{(2)}_i
      + 2\hat{k}^{(3)}_i + \hat{k}^{(4)}_i \right).
\end{equation}
The convective part of each evaluation is computed pseudo-spectrally in
rotational form, $\tilde u_j \partial_j \tilde u_i = -(\tilde{\mathbf{u}} \times \tilde{\boldsymbol{\omega}})_i
+ \partial_i (\tilde u_k \tilde u_k/2)$, whose gradient part $P_{il}$ annihilates; this costs
six inverse and three forward transforms. Under the Smagorinsky model the
subgrid stress term \eqref{eq:ns-fourier-sgs} costs six further inverse
transforms, one per independent component of $\tilde S_{ij}$, and six forward
transforms of $T_{ij}$; under the self-similar constant eddy viscosity it is
the diagonal multiplication \eqref{eq:ns-fourier-sgs-constant} and costs none.
The sponge term \eqref{eq:ns-fourier-sponge} reuses the physical-space velocity
already formed for the convective term and multiplies it by the tabulated $W$,
so it costs only the three forward transforms of $W \tilde u_i$. An evaluation thus
costs $24$ transforms under the Smagorinsky model and $12$ under the constant
eddy viscosity, and a step $96$ or $48$ in all. The scheme has no coefficients beyond the four weights, so nothing is
precomputed or cached.

\paragraph{Order.} The scheme is fourth order in $h$, so it satisfies the
second-order requirement \S\ref{ssec:alg-substeps} places on \textsc{Evolve}
for the Strang composition with a margin.

\subsection{Stability}\label{ssec:rk4-stability}

Applied to the linear model $\mathrm{d}\hat{\tilde u}/\mathrm{d}t = \lambda \hat{\tilde u}$,
\eqref{eq:rk4-stages}--\eqref{eq:rk4-update} multiply $\hat{\tilde u}$ by the
amplification factor
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rk4-polynomial}
  A(z) = 1 + z + \frac{z^2}{2} + \frac{z^3}{6} + \frac{z^4}{24},
  \qquad z = \lambda h,
\end{equation}
the degree-four Taylor polynomial of $e^{z}$, and the step is stable while
$|A(z)| \le 1$.

\paragraph{The advective limit.} The convective term is, locally, a
transport at speed $|\tilde{\mathbf{u}}|$, whose eigenvalues on the retained band lie
on the imaginary axis with $|\lambda| \le \max|\tilde{\mathbf{u}}|\, \kappa_{\max}$, where
$\kappa_{\max} = 2\pi (N/3)/H$ is the top mode retained by the $2/3$ rule. Along
the imaginary axis $|A(z)| \le 1$ holds for $|z| \le 2\sqrt{2}$, so
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rk4-advective-limit}
  h\, \max|\tilde{\mathbf{u}}|\, \kappa_{\max} \le 2\sqrt{2} .
\end{equation}
The self-similar frame pins $\max|\tilde{\mathbf{u}}| = O(1)$ on average, so the
limit does not drift over a run as it would in a decaying simulation in
physical variables. It does not pin it pointwise in time, however: the field
can momentarily concentrate into a high-amplitude structure whose
$\max|\tilde{\mathbf{u}}|$ carries a step that is stable in the settled regime past
\eqref{eq:rk4-advective-limit}.

\paragraph{The sponge limit.} The sponge forcing \eqref{eq:ns-sponge-force}
is, pointwise, a linear decay at rate $W(r) \in [0, W_{\max}]$, whose
eigenvalues lie on the negative real axis with $|\lambda| \le W_{\max}$. Along
the negative real axis $|A(z)| \le 1$ holds for $-2.785 \lesssim z \le 0$, so
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rk4-sponge-limit}
  h\, W_{\max} \le 2.785 .
\end{equation}
Unlike \eqref{eq:rk4-advective-limit}, this limit does not depend on the state:
it is fixed by the run parameter $W_{\max}$. It is not enforced by
\textsc{MaxStableDt}, but assumed. At the default $W_{\max} = 10$ and a
CFL-limited step of $h \approx 0.05$, $h\, W_{\max} \approx 0.5$, well inside
both \eqref{eq:rk4-sponge-limit} and the range $h\, W_{\max} \lesssim 1$ in
which the step also follows the exponential decay $e^{-W h}$ accurately. A run
with a larger $W_{\max}$ or a longer step must check
\eqref{eq:rk4-sponge-limit} by hand.

\paragraph{The diffusive limit.} Under the self-similar constant eddy
viscosity, the subgrid stress term \eqref{eq:ns-fourier-sgs-constant} is
marched explicitly with the rest of $R_i$. It is a linear decay at rate
$C_\nu \Delta^2 \kappa^2$, whose eigenvalues lie on the negative real axis with
$|\lambda| \le C_\nu \Delta^2 \kappa_{\max}^2$, so by the same bound as
\eqref{eq:rk4-sponge-limit}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rk4-diffusive-limit}
  h\, C_\nu \Delta^2 \kappa_{\max}^2 \le 2.785 .
\end{equation}
With $\Delta = \Delta x = H/N$ and $\kappa_{\max} = 2\pi (N/3)/H$, the product
$\Delta\, \kappa_{\max} = 2\pi/3$ is independent of the grid, and the limit becomes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rk4-diffusive-limit-cnu}
  h\, C_\nu \le \frac{9 \times 2.785}{4\pi^2} \approx 0.635 .
\end{equation}
Like the sponge limit it does not depend on the state, since in the
self-similar frame $\nu_{\mathrm{t}} = C_\nu \Delta^2$ is fixed for the run,
and because the eddy viscosity scales with $\Delta^2$ it does not tighten
under grid refinement either. It is not enforced by \textsc{MaxStableDt}, but
assumed, and can be guaranteed by choosing $\Delta t_{\max} \le 0.635/C_\nu$.
At a CFL-limited step of $h \approx 0.05$ it holds for $C_\nu \lesssim 12.7$.

\paragraph{The CFL time step.} The advective limit is therefore enforced, by the
step rule \eqref{eq:alg-dt-rule} of \S\ref{ssec:alg-timestep}. Its CFL term,
\textsc{MaxStableDt}, is the step that puts
\eqref{eq:rk4-advective-limit} at the fraction $C$ of its limit,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rk4-cfl-dt}
  \Delta t_{\mathrm{CFL}}(\tilde u; C) = C\, \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{\max|\tilde{\mathbf{u}}|\, \kappa_{\max}},
\end{equation}
and the realized CFL \eqref{eq:alg-realized-cfl} is
$\Delta t_k \max|\tilde{\mathbf{u}}_{k+1}|\, \kappa_{\max} / 2\sqrt{2}$.

\paragraph{A resolution margin.} A structure that concentrates below the
grid scale also raises $\max|\tilde{\mathbf{u}}|$, and so looks like a CFL problem;
but a smaller step only delays it. To tell the two apart the run also records
the fraction of the dealiased energy $\tfrac12 \sum |\hat{\tilde u}|^2$ carried by
$|\boldsymbol{\kappa}| > \tfrac23 \kappa_{\max}$. It stays small while the field is
resolved and rises as energy piles up against the $2/3$ cutoff. A realized CFL
excursion with a small tail fraction is a time-step problem; one preceded by a
rising tail fraction is a resolution problem.

\subsection{Solenoidality}\label{ssec:rk4-divergence}

The step contributes nothing of its own to the continuity residual. Every
contribution to $R_i$ --- convective, subgrid, and sponge alike --- has had
$P_{il}$ applied,
and $\kappa_i P_{il} = \kappa_l - \kappa_l \kappa^2/\kappa^2 = 0$, so contracting \eqref{eq:rk4-rhs}
with $\kappa_i$ gives $\kappa_i R_i(\hat{\tilde u}) = 0$. Each stage of \eqref{eq:rk4-stages}
is then divergence free, and the divergence of each mode is carried through the
step unchanged,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rk4-divergence}
  \kappa_i \hat{\tilde u}_i^{(n+1)} = \kappa_i \hat{\tilde u}_i^{(n)} .
\end{equation}
Whatever divergence enters the field is therefore neither amplified nor
damped by the step, and if \eqref{eq:ns-fourier-continuity} holds on input it
holds on output. The residual
the run log records is therefore entirely the boundary clamp's, when the clamp
is enabled (\S\ref{ssec:ns-boundary}), and not the time stepper's.

\subsection{Dealiasing}\label{ssec:rk4-dealiasing}

The $2/3$ mask of \S\ref{ssec:ns-fourier} is applied to the incoming field
before the first stage. No stage then leaves the retained band: the convective,
subgrid stress, and sponge terms are masked after their forward transforms, the
projection $P_{il}$ is diagonal in $\boldsymbol{\kappa}$, and each stage argument in
\eqref{eq:rk4-stages} is a linear combination of band-limited fields. The mask
is applied once more to $\hat{\tilde u}^{(n+1)}$, which changes nothing in exact
arithmetic but makes explicit that no content reaches the band the next scaling
step will stretch.


\section{Statistical error of time averages}\label{app:stat-error}

This appendix sets out how the statistical error of a time-averaged quantity
such as \eqref{eq:ns-pp-time-average} is estimated, following
\cite{shirian2023convergence}. Let $q(t)$ be a statistically stationary signal,
here the nondimensional subgrid transfer at the origin, $q(t) = \Pi(0, t)$ of
\eqref{eq:ns-sgs-transfer-models},
or the resolved kinetic energy there, $q(t) = \tilde k(0, t)$ of
\eqref{eq:ns-kinetic-energy-nondim},
both in the self-similar frame, with mean $\langle q \rangle$, fluctuation
$q' = q - \langle q \rangle$, and root-mean-square fluctuation $q'_{\mathrm{rms}}$.
Because the clock \eqref{eq:ns-clock} is the advective time scale $L/U$, the
self-similar time $t$ is already measured in eddy turnover times.

\subsection{Batch means}\label{ssec:stat-batch-means}

Successive values of $q$ are correlated, so the samples of a run cannot be
treated as independent. We instead use the method of batch means:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Discard the initial transient, during which the flow approaches the
  self-similar state, and let $T$ be the length of the remaining record.
  \item Split the record into $M = T/T_w$ non-overlapping windows of length
  $T_w$, and compute the window means
  \begin{equation}\label{eq:stat-window-mean}
    \langle q \rangle_i = \frac{1}{T_w} \int_{t_i}^{t_i + T_w} q(t)\, \mathrm{d}t ,
    \qquad i = 1, \dots, M .
  \end{equation}
  The samples $q_k = q(t_k)$ are recorded once per step, and under the CFL time
  step of \S\ref{ssec:alg-timestep} the $t_k$ are unequally spaced. Each
  integral \eqref{eq:stat-window-mean} is therefore evaluated as the exact
  integral of the piecewise-linear interpolant of the samples: the trapezoid
  rule on $(t_k, q_k)$, with $q$ interpolated linearly to window edges that
  fall between samples. The windows then tile $[t_0, t_0 + M T_w]$ exactly,
  and the remainder of the record shorter than $T_w$ is dropped. The plain
  mean of the samples in a window would not do: it weights each sample by one
  rather than by its $\Delta t_k$, and since $\Delta t_k \propto 1/\max|u|$ is
  correlated with the flow, the resulting mean is biased.
  \item Estimate the mean and its standard error (SEM) as
  \begin{equation}\label{eq:stat-sem}
    q_{\mathrm{est}} = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^{M} \langle q \rangle_i ,
    \qquad
    \mathrm{SEM} \simeq \frac{\mathrm{STD}(\langle q \rangle_i)}{\sqrt{M}} .
  \end{equation}
  \item Report the $95\%$ confidence interval
  $q_{\mathrm{est}} \pm 1.96\,\mathrm{SEM}$.
\end{enumerate}
The confidence interval assumes a Gaussian error, which the central limit
theorem supplies when the windows are uncorrelated. Equation \eqref{eq:stat-sem}
holds only if
\begin{enumerate}
  \item the windows are uncorrelated, $T_w \gg \tau_{\mathrm{corr}}$, where
  $\tau_{\mathrm{corr}}$ is the integral time scale of $q$; and
  \item there are enough windows, $M \gg 1$.
\end{enumerate}
For a fixed $T$ the two conditions pull in opposite directions, so $T_w$ has to
be chosen with care.

\subsection{Choosing the window length}\label{ssec:stat-window}

The window length is chosen by plotting $\mathrm{SEM}\sqrt{T}$ against $T_w$,
both in units of the eddy turnover time. The curve has three regimes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Small $T_w$.} Adjacent windows are correlated, so $M$ overcounts
  the independent samples and
  $\mathrm{SEM} \approx q'_{\mathrm{rms}} \sqrt{T_w / T}$, a slope of $1/2$
  on log axes. This underestimates the true error.
  \item \emph{Plateau.} Both conditions hold and
  $\mathrm{SEM} \approx q'_{\mathrm{rms}} \sqrt{\tau_{\mathrm{corr}} / T}$,
  independent of $T_w$. This is the true SEM.
  \item \emph{Large $T_w$.} $M$ is too small and the estimate is noisy and
  unreliable.
\end{itemize}
The small-$T_w$ asymptote and the plateau intersect at
$T_w = \tau_{\mathrm{corr}}$, where
\begin{equation}\label{eq:stat-tau-corr}
  \tau_{\mathrm{corr}} = \frac{1}{q'^2_{\mathrm{rms}}}
  \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} C(\tau)\, \mathrm{d}\tau ,
  \qquad
  C(\tau) = \langle q'(t)\, q'(t + \tau) \rangle ,
\end{equation}
so the plot also yields the integral time scale. A clear plateau requires a
long record: \cite{shirian2023convergence} found one only for
$T \gtrsim 10^4$ eddy turnover times, approximately $1.6 \times 10^4$ for
homogeneous isotropic turbulence and $1.4 \times 10^4$ for channel flow at
$Re_\tau = 180$. A dissipation-type quantity, being a second-order statistic of
the velocity gradients, has $\tau_{\mathrm{corr}}$ of roughly five eddy
turnover times in homogeneous isotropic turbulence.

